        "math_academy_id": "q-88585",
        "problem": "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}\u2061({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.",
        "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}\u2061({x}^{4})$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3\\operatorname{tan}\u2061((-x)^{4}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}\u2061({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}\u2061({x}^{4})$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}\u2061({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}\u2061({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
